A||||6||batboy||code 35 and code 42||||1||
Z||000000||batboy||04-12-2000||06:16 PM||||On my 85 GT V6, I have a exhaust manifold leaking. Soon after that happened, the car began running rough with a fluctuating tach needle when first cold started in the morning. Once it warms up and I'm out on the highway, everything is fine. I figured the two were related.<P>Once during this rough running period, the check engine light came on. This morning it wouldn't start. Engine cranked and would cough and sputter and almost run, but not quite. I checked the codes and it flashed a 35 (idle air control) and a 42 (electronic spark timing).<P>Somebody save me hours of searching and reading past Forum posts and maybe give me an idea of what to check for first. I'm beginning to suspect that the leaking manifold and rough running on start up are unrelated now. Ogre has said in the past that the throttle position sensor is a common problem. I will welcome any suggestions or guesses. Also, what is an idle air control?||||reg||1
Z||000001||DRH||04-12-2000||06:57 PM||||Quick guesses here...<P>42 means it is not getting a proper signal from the distributor. Most likely the pickup coil (the little coil at the top of the distributor shaft), the ignition module (the little black box inside the distributor), or loose connection in the wiring.<P>The idle air control valve is mounted on the throttle body, has four wires going to it. The ECM moves the valve in and out to regulate the amount of air allowed around the throttle plate to control idle speed. The code 35 simply means the ECM has detected idle outside the acceptable range it is trying to set to. In your case this could very well be a "false" code caused by the code 42 problem above.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-12-2000||07:01 PM||||check the distributor then worry about the IAC. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||04-12-2000||07:18 PM||||Should I just go ahead and replace the ignition pickup and module, since they're fairly cheap and do go bad occasionally? I have a new cap and rotor that I never got around to replacing too. Maybe I'll do all that, reset the ECM and see what happens. I still have to get after that exhaust leak soon too. Thanks for the suggestions. Further comments are welcome.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||04-25-2000||08:17 PM||||Update! The 85 GT finally wouldn't start one morning, so after parking it for a few days, I took the distributor cap off and found the center coil terminal completely corroded away and the rotor didn't look so good either. I installed the new cap and rotor that have been rattling around in the trunk for a couple months. After a brief chant to the Fiero deities, I turned the key in the ignition. It fired right up and ran smooth. Hallelujah! Life is good when it's a simple problem that you already had the parts for.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||04-25-2000||08:19 PM||||I was being a good little bat and checking the distributor first, just like Ogre said.||||reg||10
Z||000006||Ozzy||04-26-2000||12:47 AM||||And I quote; "After a brief chant to the Fiero deities..."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Gotta luv that! Oh how I can  relate? Let me count thy ways!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Ozzy||||reg||10
